<speak>
<p>We want to describe a row whose positions form a cycle. If an entry moves past the last position, it should return to the first. Ordinary polynomials do not do that automatically, so let us change the rules to match the circle.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>Take the cyclic group generated by X, with the rule X to the n equals one. Its elements are one, X, X squared, through X to the n minus one. X is a formal marker for one step around the cycle, rather than a number we are substituting.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The integer group ring attaches an integer coefficient to each position and adds the terms. We add coefficients as usual, and multiply with the rule X to the n equals one. This is the quotient of integer polynomials by X to the n minus one. In the quotient, that defining polynomial is zero.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>For n equal to four, X to the fourth is one, X to the fifth is X, and X inverse is X cubed. For example, three X to the fifth becomes three X: the exponent wraps once around the four positions. A negative exponent moves backward around the same cycle.<break time="800ms"/></p>
<p>Every element has a representative of degree less than n. Replace X to the n by one, X to the n plus one by X, and keep reducing exponents. Collect the terms whose exponents land in the same position.<break time="700ms"/></p>
<p>The reduced representative is also unique. If two polynomials of degree less than n represent the same element, their difference is a multiple of X to the n minus one. But any nonzero such multiple has degree at least n. The difference must therefore be zero.<break time="900ms"/></p>
<p>Thus the ordered coefficient list is exactly the same information as one group ring element with degree less than n. Its positions are unambiguous. Next, we will see why multiplying this polynomial by X is precisely a right cyclic shift of the row.<break time="900ms"/></p>
</speak>
